  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to understand the properties of a self-sustaining function \\( h \\). Specifically, we need to find the values of \\( h(x) \\) such that the product \\( \\prod_{i=1}^{10} h(x_i) \\) is maximized while satisfying the self-sustaining condition \\( h(x) \\cdot h(y) \\geq x \\cdot y \\) for all \\( x, y \\).\n\nTo simplify the self-sustaining condition, note that \\( h(1) \\) must be at least 1, since \\( h(1) \\cdot h(1) \\geq 1 \\). Also, for larger values of \\( x \\), \\( h(x) \\) must be particularly large to dominate the product.\n\nLet's consider the critical cases:\n1. \\( h(1) = k \\). For \\( k \\) to be maximized, it should be around \\( \\sqrt{10 \\cdot 19} \\) (by the AM-GM inequality, \\( \\frac{k + k}{2} \\geq \\sqrt{k^2} = k \\)), which gives \\( k = \\sqrt{190} \\approx 13.8 \\). Since \\( h \\) is an integer function, we can set \\( h(1) = 14 \\).\n2. For \\( h(2) \\), \\( h(2) \\cdot 14 \\geq 2 \\), so \\( h(2) \\geq \\frac{2}{14} = \\frac{1}{7} \\). The smallest integer greater than \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is 1.\n3. For \\( h(2) = 1 \\), \\( h(2) \\cdot 14 \\geq 2 \\), so \\( h(2) = 1 \\). This gives \\( h(2) = 1 \\).\n4. Similarly, for \\( h(x) \\), \\( h(x) \\) should be larger than \\( \\frac{x}{h(1)} \\) to contribute significantly to the product.\n\nBy testing such values, we find the maximum possible value for \\( h(7) \\) to be 7. This satisfies the self-sustaining condition \\( h(7) \\cdot h(1) \\geq 7 \\cdot 1 \\), since \\( h(1) = 14 \\) and \\( h(7) = 7 \\).\n\nTherefore, the maximum possible value for \\( h(7) \\) is 7.",
